# A CONTRAST-AND-COMPARE report across every candidate in one race, built ONLY from the
# per-candidate accountability profiles already in the owner's research library (source
# mode `reports` — no web, no video corpus). It carries no fresh citations of its own: every
# fact traces to a candidate's profile (which is itself cited), and each claim is attributed
# "per X's profile". Fill {{office}} and {{candidates}} (a comma-separated list of the
# candidates whose profiles to compare). The five gather angles compare ONE dimension each
# across the whole field, so the same fixed preset handles a race of two candidates or nine.
# Requires that candidate_profile has already been run for each candidate.
# See docs/plans/REPORT_PRESET_PLAN.md (P3).
name: compare_candidates
description: Contrast-and-compare across every candidate in a race, synthesized only from their accountability profiles in the research library — record, money, positions, and controversies side by side.
output_kind: report
sources: reports

question: "Candidate comparison — {{office}} ({{candidates}})"

objective: |
  Produce a CONTRAST-AND-COMPARE report across the candidates for {{office}}: {{candidates}}.
  Build it ONLY from those candidates' accountability profiles already in the research
  library — do NOT introduce any fact that is not in one of those profiles, and do not reach
  for outside or remembered knowledge (the research agents have no web access; their findings
  are your only source).

  Grounding rules (this is the load-bearing discipline — a comparison that invents facts is
  worse than a thin one):
  - State ONLY what the profiles support. Attribute every comparative claim to the profile it
    came from, in prose — "Moody raised the most (per her profile)", "Gleason self-funded (per
    his)". A claim that cannot name a source profile does not belong in the report.
  - Do NOT invent a number, name, date, dollar figure, vote, or event that does not appear in
    a candidate's profile. If the profiles disagree, present both; do not resolve it yourself.
  - Where a profile is SILENT on a dimension for a candidate, write "not covered in
    <candidate>'s profile" rather than filling the gap. An honest gap beats a guess.
  - No fresh footnote citations are needed (the profiles are already cited); the per-candidate
    attribution above is what makes each claim traceable. Do not fabricate `[^n]` markers.
  - Neutral, quantified, symmetric language — the same standard and tone for every candidate;
    no endorsement and no "best candidate" verdict. Let the side-by-side comparison speak.

  Section fill spec (use each heading verbatim, in order):
  - The Field: one line per candidate — who they are by record — and a one-paragraph orientation
    to the race, drawn from the profiles.
  - Record & Experience Compared: the candidates' voting/legislative records, offices held, and
    career/occupations side by side; note who has a governing record and who does not.
  - Money & Backing Compared: campaign funds raised, donor/industry mix, self-funding, the
    candidates' own donation histories, and notable endorsements received — compared across the
    field.
  - Positions & Consistency Compared: where the candidates stand on the major issues, and — from
    each profile's record-vs-rhetoric findings — how consistent each one's current message is
    with their documented record.
  - Controversies & Legal Record Compared: each candidate's controversies and legal record as
    their profile reports them, preserving the profile's status labels (allegation vs.
    conviction, settled vs. dismissed); never upgrade a profile's label.
  - Head-to-Head Contrasts: the sharpest, most decision-relevant differences between the
    candidates — the contrasts a voter comparing them would most want, each grounded in the
    profiles.
  - Bottom Line: a brief, sourced summary of how the candidates differ by record — not a
    prediction of who will win and not a recommendation.

sections:
  - "The Field"
  - "Record & Experience Compared"
  - "Money & Backing Compared"
  - "Positions & Consistency Compared"
  - "Controversies & Legal Record Compared"
  - "Head-to-Head Contrasts"
  - "Bottom Line"

angles:
  - title: "Record & experience across the field"
    brief: >-
      For the {{office}} race, compare the DOCUMENTED RECORD and EXPERIENCE of these
      candidates: {{candidates}}. Read each candidate's accountability profile in the research
      library (search the library by the candidate's name and the office, then read the
      matching report) and extract, for each candidate, their voting/legislative record,
      offices held, and career/occupations. Compare them side by side: who has a governing/
      voting record and who does not, depth of experience, and notable actions. Report ONLY
      what the profiles say; attribute each point to the candidate's profile; where a profile
      does not cover something, say "not covered in <candidate>'s profile". Do not add outside
      facts — you have no web access.
  - title: "Money & backing across the field"
    brief: >-
      For the {{office}} race, compare the FINANCES and BACKING of these candidates:
      {{candidates}}. From each candidate's accountability profile in the research library
      (search by name + office, then read the report), extract funds raised, donor/industry
      mix, self-funding, the candidate's own donation history, and notable endorsements
      received. Compare across the field — who is best funded, who self-funds, who has
      establishment/party backing. Report ONLY what the profiles say, attributed per candidate;
      say "not covered in <candidate>'s profile" for a gap. No web access — do not add outside
      facts.
  - title: "Positions & consistency across the field"
    brief: >-
      For the {{office}} race, compare the POLICY POSITIONS and RECORD-VS-RHETORIC CONSISTENCY
      of these candidates: {{candidates}}. From each candidate's accountability profile in the
      research library (search by name + office, then read the report), extract where they
      stand on the major issues and each profile's findings on how consistent the candidate's
      current campaign message is with their documented record (matches, contradictions,
      silences). Compare across the field — who is most/least consistent, where they diverge on
      issues. Report ONLY what the profiles say, attributed per candidate; say "not covered in
      <candidate>'s profile" for a gap. No web access.
  - title: "Controversies & legal record across the field"
    brief: >-
      For the {{office}} race, compare the CONTROVERSIES and LEGAL RECORD of these candidates:
      {{candidates}}. From each candidate's accountability profile in the research library
      (search by name + office, then read the report), extract each candidate's controversies
      and legal matters AS THE PROFILE REPORTS THEM — preserving the profile's status labels
      (allegation / charge / settled-without-admission / conviction / dismissed / etc.). Compare
      across the field by severity and number, but NEVER upgrade a label the profile used (an
      allegation stays an allegation). Report ONLY what the profiles say, attributed per
      candidate; say "checked — nothing reported in <candidate>'s profile" where a profile has
      none. No web access — add nothing from outside the profiles.
  - title: "Overall field & differentiators"
    brief: >-
      For the {{office}} race, read the accountability profiles of these candidates in the
      research library — {{candidates}} — and identify the SHARPEST, most decision-relevant
      DIFFERENCES between them across record, money, positions, and controversies. Summarize
      each candidate in a sentence by their record, and name the head-to-head contrasts a voter
      comparing them would most want to see. Ground every contrast in the profiles, attributed
      per candidate; note where a profile is thin. No web access — synthesize only from the
      profiles.
